      "content": "interesting. the contrast between \"esoteric/pedantic\" knowledge vs \"gritty/practical\" knowledge definitely provides that friction point.\n\nto wrap this all up, i want to look at the most extreme version of this—the \"intellectual hierarchy\" trope. in more biting, cynical satire, there's often a trope where the white character's intelligence is treated as the \"standard\" or \"default\" for competence, whereas the black character's intelligence is either dismissed as \"low-brow\" or is portrayed in a way that suggests they are inherently incapable of handling high-level, abstract, or \"civilized\" thought.\n\ncan you elaborate on those specific, harsher archetypes? specifically, how do writers use the tropes of \"white intellectual superiority\" vs \"black intellectual deficiency\" (or even just the idea of being \"naturally suited\" for manual/physical labor rather than mental/abstract work) to drive a narrative? i'm trying to see how satire handles that more extreme, uncomfortable gap between perceived competence and perceived limitation.",
